WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.080
Freddy Kreuger has been haunting the dreams of&nbsp;
teenagers since 1984, but the foul-mouthed serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.201
killer didn't just appear out of thin air. And&nbsp;
movie teens aren't the only nightmare victims.

00:00:11.201 --> 00:00:14.081
"Welcome to my nightmare."

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.640
Director Wes Craven might have dreamed up Freddy,&nbsp;
but it took a whole team of people to bring him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.400
to life. The physical process of transforming&nbsp;
actor Robert Englund into Freddy Krueger with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.640
layers of latex, tubes, and the occasional&nbsp;
goat bladder took around three-and-a-half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.840
to four hours. Going through that lengthy&nbsp;
makeup application process helped Englund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.840
get into the headspace to inhabit Freddy — and&nbsp;
he drew inspiration from classic movie monsters.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.360
Englund grew up in Los Angeles, surrounded&nbsp;
by the remnants of Old Hollywood. As a child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.000
he would accompany his parents&nbsp;
to major studio previews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.200
where he would see first-run films that are&nbsp;
considered today's classics like "Anatomy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.040
a Murder" and "The Guns of Navarone."&nbsp;
It makes sense that Englund looked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.280
the past when developing his own soon-to-be&nbsp;
classic character: as he told The Guardian —

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.760
"My Freddy was inspired by Klaus&nbsp;
Kinski's 'Nosferatu,' but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.240
by all the monsters that Lon Chaney created."

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.760
Freddy's signature slouch&nbsp;
was born out of necessity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.880
but it also had a less sinister inspiration:&nbsp;
actor James Cagney. Englund continued —

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.640
"The way Freddy always stands with his legs&nbsp;
far apart was something I stole from James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.640
Cagney — a pose that signifies sheer power.&nbsp;
People wonder why Freddy has this strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.560
posture with his right shoulder slouched,&nbsp;
but it was because that claw was so heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.200
it weighed me down. But I liked the way it&nbsp;
made me look like a cowboy drawing a gun."

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.000
As Wes Craven began to conceptualize&nbsp;
Freddy's look, he knew one thing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.480
famous slasher villains: they all seem to&nbsp;
have some type of mask. For Michael Myers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.040
it's a mask of William Shatner's face&nbsp;
that's been painted white. In "Friday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.040
the 13th," Jason Voorhees hides behind&nbsp;
a hockey mask, and Leatherface from "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.080
Texas Chain Saw Massacre" wears the&nbsp;
face of one of his unlucky victims.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.320
According to Cinefantastique,&nbsp;
Craven was taken with the ancient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.160
mythological concept of the mask. He explained —

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.760
"Almost all cultures have it in their&nbsp;
most important ceremonies. It kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.080
is a combination of removing humanity and&nbsp;
yet leaving it there, behind the eyes."

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.680
For Craven, who hoped to imbue his&nbsp;
nightmare with a little more personality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.480
the idea of a mask presented a&nbsp;
unique problem. As he told Vulture —

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.560
"I wanted my villain to have a 'mask,' but&nbsp;
be able to talk and taunt and threaten."

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.960
To pull it off, Craven settled on scar tissue&nbsp;
from burns that creates a type of mask without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:22.520
obscuring Freddy's ability to emote and express&nbsp;
his feelings. Thus, Freddy's iconic look was born.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:27.600
"The only thing to fear… is fear himself!"

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.840
Once Wes Craven had designed Freddy Krueger,&nbsp;
he had to dress him — but this also presented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.520
a unique problem. For a shapeshifting&nbsp;
nightmare spirit, the filmmaker had to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.520
up with something so recognizable that viewers&nbsp;
would understand instantly they were looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.280
at Freddy even when he wasn't in his standard&nbsp;
form. Craven seemed to instinctively understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.680
something branding experts harp on about today&nbsp;
— that the right color scheme can create a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.360
so recognizable that audiences automatically&nbsp;
associate it with the thing they're meant to.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.400
Speaking to Cinefantastique, Craven said he wanted&nbsp;
Freddy's costume to be obvious no matter what form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.760
he shape-shifted into. He was inspired by the DC&nbsp;
Comics hero Plastic Man: as the director put it —

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.040
"He used to change shape, but you could&nbsp;
always tell it was him. [...] So I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.240
Freddy to be a shape-shifter that&nbsp;
could be recognized from his colors."

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.600
To find the right palette, the filmmaker&nbsp;
didn't have to look much further than his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.280
coffee table. Craven read a 1982 issue of&nbsp;
Scientific American that reported that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.560
to the human retina, the most&nbsp;
contrasting colors are red and green.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.400
Every good slasher villain has a signature weapon&nbsp;
— Leatherface has his chainsaw, Jason wields a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.840
machete, and Michael Myers uses a kitchen knife.&nbsp;
Understanding the importance of an eye-catching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.000
weapon, Wes Craven wanted to give Freddy something&nbsp;
that tapped into his audience's most primal fears.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.360
It was Craven's cat that got him thinking about&nbsp;
built-in weapon hands, as he later explained —

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.520
"I wanted Freddy's weapon to be&nbsp;
something somebody 1,000 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.560
ago could have related to. [...] Predators&nbsp;
like cave bears and saber-tooth tigers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.920
all those creatures — if you look at them,&nbsp;
they're just knives, hands full of knives."

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.600
Craven believed that primal fear&nbsp;
still lurks in the human subconscious.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.960
The filmmaker and his team played around with&nbsp;
possible knives to use, considering fishing knives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.640
before landing on an odd-looking steak knife that&nbsp;
was flipped around and given another sharp edge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.280
By the end of the production, there were three&nbsp;
versions of the glove with different scenes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.640
uses in mind: the glove with real blades in it, a&nbsp;
glove with Mylar-covered blades to reflect light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.560
and a hard rubber glove for performing&nbsp;
stunts. As Robert Englund reflected —

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.240
"My running joke was I had to be careful going&nbsp;
to the bathroom or I could change my religion."

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.303
"Why don't you… reach out and cut someone?"

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.880
It's long been said that you should write&nbsp;
what you know, and that's a sentiment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.840
Wes Craven took to heart as he developed&nbsp;
his film. Aspects of the dream-stalking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.880
predator were inspired by a freaky encounter&nbsp;
that he had as a child through his window.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.760
Fedoras were a popular menswear accessory&nbsp;
during Craven's childhood, but one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.720
in particular, left a mark on his young&nbsp;
mind. While young Wesley was sleeping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.120
he was awoken by a seriously intoxicated man on&nbsp;
the street outside his window. Craven recounted —

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.400
"He just did a mind-f--- on me. He just&nbsp;
basically somehow knew I was up there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.320
and he looked right into my eyes."

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.560
Terrified, the child hid in his room for what&nbsp;
seemed like forever. When it felt safe again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.520
he crept to the window again — only to see&nbsp;
the man still standing there before walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.640
in the direction of his apartment entrance.&nbsp;
Although the encounter ended without incidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.720
it stuck with Craven — especially&nbsp;
the fact that the creepy guy was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.240
wearing a fedora. He decided to give his&nbsp;
monster the same hat: as he asserted,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.160
"The idea of an adult who was frightening and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.520
enjoyed terrifying a child&nbsp;
was the origin of Freddy."

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.520
After his casting as Freddy Krueger, Robert&nbsp;
Englund went on to become a beloved character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.880
actor with a penchant for villainy, appearing&nbsp;
in more than 75 feature films through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:36.080
years. It's hard to believe that if not for&nbsp;
a fluke of scheduling, Englund's notoriety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.000
as an internationally recognizable&nbsp;
movie monster might not have happened.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.280
A classically trained actor, Englund auditioned&nbsp;
for the part of Luke Skywalker in 1976. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.520
his unsuccessful audition, he came home,&nbsp;
drank a six-pack of Heineken, and told his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.280
couch-surfing pal Mark Hamill about the gig.&nbsp;
While Hamill's career took off as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:59.160
Englund was still struggling to get past being&nbsp;
typecast as the nerdy character. In 1983,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.760
he landed a more prominent role as the lovable&nbsp;
alien resistance fighter Willie on the miniseries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:09.040
"V," ultimately getting promoted to a regular&nbsp;
cast member in its sequels and 1984 series.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.280
Englund was happy enough with the&nbsp;
success of the series, telling Vulture —

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.440
"I was on the cusp — or thought I was on&nbsp;
the cusp — of celebrity, the result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.960
starring as an adorable curly-haired&nbsp;
alien in the miniseries 'V' on NBC."

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.160
According to Englund, he went in for "A&nbsp;
Nightmare on Elm Street" because it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:28.240
the only audition that fit into his schedule&nbsp;
during the hiatus. Impressed with Englund's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.920
take on Freddy's darker side, Craven gave&nbsp;
him the role — and the rest is history.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:37.520
"We got special work to do here, you and me."

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.000
Internet rumors have long claimed that Freddy&nbsp;
was based on a real-world villain, from a 2017&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.520
Facebook viral claim that Freddy hailed from 1800s&nbsp;
Ohio to a 2020 version declaring the villain comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.320
from Oklahoma. While these rumors amount to little&nbsp;
more than creepypasta, the story itself does draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.720
some inspiration from a real-life terror. In&nbsp;
Vulture's oral history of "A Nightmare on Elm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.880
Street," Wes Craven explained another origin for&nbsp;
his deadly nightmare tale: a Los Angeles Times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:07.120
piece about an immigrant family who lost their&nbsp;
son under unusually horrifying circumstances.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:11.400
The family — which had survived Cambodia's&nbsp;
Killing Fields under the Khmer Rouge regime — had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.200
immigrated to the United States to move&nbsp;
on with their lives as best they could.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:19.440
But they found this difficult to do when their&nbsp;
young son was plagued by terrifying nightmares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.160
and he ultimately died in the middle of&nbsp;
one. According to Craven, the child was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:27.480
worried that if he fell asleep, the dream&nbsp;
monster would kill him. The director mused —

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.760
"Here was a youngster having a vision&nbsp;
of a horror that everyone older was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.600
denying. That became the central&nbsp;
line of 'Nightmare on Elm Street.'"

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.640
According to a 1987 Los Angeles Times item,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:42.000
the anomaly wasn't a one-off — it happened&nbsp;
so frequently that experts gave it the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:47.720
"Asian Death Syndrome." The Times claimed&nbsp;
that from April 1983 to January 1987,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:53.560
upwards of 130 refugees fleeing Southeast&nbsp;
Asia had died from the mysterious condition.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.480
While we don't normally think of&nbsp;
acting as a treacherous career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:00.840
on-set accidents can happen — especially&nbsp;
when there are special effects involved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.680
From "Twilight Zone: The Movie"&nbsp;
to "The Crow," there's a shocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.040
number of horrific tragedies that have&nbsp;
taken place during film production.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.520
Fortunately for fans of Robert Englund,&nbsp;
things didn't turn out nearly as bad as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.040
they could have on the set of "A Nightmare&nbsp;
on Elm Street," when one crucial scene didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:18.760
go as expected. In a tale recounted by several&nbsp;
members of the cast and crew to Rolling Stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.480
Englund surmised the special effects team&nbsp;
had been shooting for about four days when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.160
it came time to film Johnny Depp's death&nbsp;
scene — and he speculated that they may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.600
or may not have been under the influence of a&nbsp;
certain white powder. For the bloody sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:35.040
they devised a system to shoot out hundreds&nbsp;
of gallons of red water all over the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.120
but they overlooked the insulation on their&nbsp;
electrical components. An effects technician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.240
went the wrong way, creating a "two-inch tsunami"&nbsp;
of fake blood all over the electrical equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:49.480
Sparks flew, the lights went out, and Englund took&nbsp;
off with Heather Langenkamp. As the actor joked —

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:53.640
"I wasn't about to f---ing save anybody.&nbsp;
I didn't want to get electrocuted."

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:58.800
"Now I'm playin' with power!"

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.200
Freddy might be one of our favorite villains of&nbsp;
all time, but few people would ever want to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:08.000
him in a dark alley — even if it means getting&nbsp;
to see one of his coolest tricks. In one infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.440
scene in the original "Nightmare on Elm Street,"&nbsp;
Tina steps into the alley and runs into Freddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:16.640
who stretches out his arms before chasing&nbsp;
her around and then finally killing her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:19.800
As far as slasher death scenes go,&nbsp;
it's one of the greats — thanks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:23.280
large part to some pretty spectacular&nbsp;
work on the part of the effects team.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.760
These days, using CGI to create special effects&nbsp;
in movies is something of a given. But back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:32.280
the golden age of slasher films, filmmakers had&nbsp;
to get a little more creative — doubly so in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:36.440
film series that deals in the surreal world of&nbsp;
nightmares where the laws of physics don't quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:41.400
apply. The special effects team ended up attaching&nbsp;
fishing poles to Freddy's extra-long arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:46.080
effectively turning him into a giant marionette.&nbsp;
To make his knives spark when he touched the wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:49.720
a car battery was connected to his&nbsp;
glove. And for the scene's final feat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:52.600
the special effects team used a&nbsp;
revolving room like the one Fred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:57.334
Astaire uses to dance across the ceiling&nbsp;
in the 1951 musical "Royal Wedding."

